      "transcript": "Harriet Saye: Regatta volunteers, look first at the river orientation. I will place the essential stations, explain controlled crossings and finish with safety messages.\nHarriet Saye: The registration tent is on the upstream meadow beside the service gate, immediately before the rope-lined spectator path begins.\nHarriet Saye: Upstream is to the left of the plan and racing crews move from left to right towards the finish.\nHarriet Saye: A missing-person description is not broadcast with a full name unless the safeguarding lead authorises that wording.\nHarriet Saye: The upstream gate closes to new spectators thirty minutes before the final, reducing movement along the narrow approach.\nHarriet Saye: The boathouse is the long riverside building set behind the central pontoon, on the bank opposite the town steps.\nHarriet Saye: Spectators remain behind the rope line; only accredited crews and launch staff use the narrow path beside the water.\nHarriet Saye: Litter teams work after each race block, when the crossing is open and movement along the bank is less restricted.\nHarriet Saye: First aid is on the town-side bank immediately upstream of the pedestrian bridge, beside the paved access route.\nHarriet Saye: The public bridge stays open, but the temporary riverside crossing is controlled because boats pass close to the bank.\nHarriet Saye: If thunder is reported, the organiser suspends racing and directs the public away from trees and temporary metal structures.\nHarriet Saye: The commentary stand faces the final two hundred metres from the town bank, just upstream of the finish tower.\nHarriet Saye: A warning bell sounds two minutes before each start, giving marshals time to clear the crossing and check the finish zone.\nHarriet Saye: Volunteer radios use earpieces near the commentary tower, preventing routine messages from distracting spectators or being mistaken for public instructions.\nHarriet Saye: The main marshal post is beside the controlled riverside crossing, where the meadow path meets the central pontoon approach.\nHarriet Saye: Rescue launches have priority over racing boats when displaying a blue flag, and volunteers do not attempt water rescues themselves.\nHarriet Saye: The finish judge alone confirms a result; marshals should never repeat an unofficial placing heard from the crowd.\nHarriet Saye: The finish pontoon projects from the town bank immediately downstream of the finish tower.\nHarriet Saye: The commentary team may request crowd information, but safety reports always take priority on the radio channel.\nHarriet Saye: Wheelchair access to the bank follows the paved route behind the boathouse rather than the steeper grass beside the registration tent.\nHarriet Saye: The crossing opens between races and closes as soon as the warning bell sounds.\nHarriet Saye: Crew trailers enter from the upstream service road and never use the pedestrian meadow gate marked on the public route.\nHarriet Saye: Keep the emergency lane clear of bags, chairs and bicycles throughout the day.\nHarriet Saye: The central pontoon is reserved for launches; racing shells approach separate landing stages under instructions from water officials.\nHarriet Saye: Food vendors receive a separate waste collection point and must not place bins inside the emergency lane.\nHarriet Saye: At shift change, incoming volunteers meet their outgoing partner at the assigned station instead of gathering near registration.\nHarriet Saye: Take a lost child to the first-aid point, where the safeguarding lead can make an announcement.\nHarriet Saye: At the finish, volunteers watch for spectators leaning across the rope because attention tends to follow the boats downstream.\nHarriet Saye: The lost-property box stays with first aid until the final race, when sealed items transfer to the event office.\nHarriet Saye: Begin every radio message with your post number, then describe the issue briefly.\nHarriet Saye: The first-aid team has a direct radio channel, but ordinary welfare enquiries should still begin at the information desk.\nHarriet Saye: Please complete a short incident log at each station even when no radio call was necessary.\nHarriet Saye: I expect every regatta post to protect the crossing, emergency lane and radio discipline until the final crews leave the river.",
