      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Urban streets accumulated horse manure, mud, market waste and dust. Hand sweeping could move this mixture into heaps for collection, but the work was slow and exposed labourers to traffic and airborne dirt. Expanding towns wanted a machine that would gather material continuously while travelling. The challenge was not merely to rotate a brush. Debris had to be lifted from an uneven surface, retained rather than scattered, and deposited somewhere that could be emptied.\n\nB Early mechanical sweepers were pulled by horses. Wheels drove a system of gears, which turned a cylindrical or diagonal brush as the vehicle moved. Setting the brush across the direction of travel pushed refuse towards a conveyor or collection body. Designers experimented with bristle stiffness and contact pressure: a brush held too high missed compacted dirt, while one forced down wore rapidly and threw dust beyond the machine's path.\n\nC A powered brush alone can leave a clean strip beside a larger ridge of rubbish. Successful machines therefore coordinated sweeping with a receptacle. Some lifted debris mechanically; others directed it into a hopper behind the brush. The mouth of the collection system had to pass close to the road without striking raised stones. Patents repeatedly altered this junction between brush, roadway and container because it determined whether the machine collected dirt or simply redistributed it.\n\nD Dry sweeping created clouds that annoyed pedestrians and reduced visibility. Water sprays were added to damp fine particles before the brush disturbed them. Too little water failed to control dust, whereas too much turned soil into a heavy slurry and increased the load. Operators learned to adjust spraying to weather and surface conditions. Dust control thus became an operational judgement as well as a mechanical feature, and water supply limited how long a sweeper could remain on its route.\n\nE Motorised vehicles removed dependence on the movement of a horse-drawn wheel and allowed brushes, conveyors and suction systems to have their own power. Greater control did not eliminate trade-offs. A fast brush could clean aggressively but wear itself and damage loose surfaces. Suction captured lighter particles yet struggled with bulky objects unless paired with side brushes. Manufacturers combined mechanisms, placing rotating brushes at the kerb to feed material towards a central intake.\n\nF Mechanisation reduced the amount of hand sweeping required along a route, but it did not remove workers from street cleaning. Drivers had to judge kerb distance, monitor water and clear blockages. Crews were still needed around parked vehicles, narrow alleys and obstacles. Maintenance shifted effort into workshops, where bristles, bearings and collection systems were repaired. The machine changed the composition of labour from repeated manual sweeping towards operation, support and specialised maintenance.\n\nG Modern sweeping schedules balance cleanliness, pollution control, traffic and cost. A machine may pass frequently on a commercial street and rarely on a quiet road, even though both use the same equipment. Sensors can show when a hopper is full, but they cannot decide which neighbourhood receives priority. Street sweepers illustrate a wider point about municipal technology: successive patents improve components, while the public outcome still depends on routing, staffing and the definition of an acceptably clean street.\n\nH Engineers evaluate a sweeper by spreading a measured mixture over a test surface and weighing what the machine recovers. Such trials allow designs to be compared, but a controlled strip cannot reproduce parked cars, broken kerbs or wet leaves. Field data are needed to connect laboratory efficiency with an operating route. Fuel use, brush replacement and missed patches all belong in that assessment; the percentage collected in one pass is only one dimension of performance. Repeated runs with worn bristles are particularly informative, since a machine purchased for impressive new-condition results will spend most of its service life somewhere below that peak.\n\nI Fine road dust may contain tyre particles, soil and residues from combustion. Capturing it before rain can reduce material entering drains, yet sweeping can also lift particles into the air if brushes and sprays are poorly adjusted. Disposal matters too, since hopper contents cannot automatically be treated as clean soil. Effective programmes follow the material from roadway to final handling instead of declaring success as soon as the pavement looks clear.",
