      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 3: When an hour becomes a currency",
      "passageTitle": "When an hour becomes a currency",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A A time bank records services in hours. A member earns credit by helping someone and spends credit when another member helps them. One hour is normally valued as one hour regardless of whether the task is tutoring, gardening or companionship. Advocates argue that this rule recognises contributions undervalued in cash markets and invites people with limited money to exchange. The equality is moral and administrative; it does not mean that every task demands identical training or effort.\n\nB Many schemes use a coordinator or digital platform rather than relying on two people to swap directly. A member can assist one person and later receive an unrelated service from someone else. This breaks the need for a coincidence of wants. It also creates work: requests must be matched, boundaries explained and inactive accounts followed up. The infrastructure supporting an apparently simple hour-for-hour exchange is substantial, even when much of it is volunteered.\n\nC Newcomers often join because they need a practical service, while established members may be motivated by social contact or a wish to contribute. Organisations sometimes participate too, offering meeting space or staff time. These groups behave differently. Someone with urgent needs may spend before earning; a regular volunteer may accumulate credits they never use; an institution can dominate the ledger through its scale. A durable bank must accommodate those patterns without turning credit balances into measures of personal worth.\n\nD Equal time is easy to defend for neighbourly assistance but harder where licensing, liability or expensive equipment is involved. A qualified electrician cannot necessarily provide regulated work through an informal exchange, and an hour using specialised machinery costs more than an hour of conversation. Some banks exclude high-risk services; others reimburse materials separately. The principle survives by narrowing what a credit promises. It measures time, not unrestricted access to every professional service.\n\nE The system's social value may lie in generalised reciprocity. A member does not owe the original helper; the obligation is to the network. This can connect people who would never form a private barter pair. Yet trust weakens if members believe some repeatedly receive without contributing. Strictly blocking negative balances can exclude those with illness or caring responsibilities, precisely the people a community scheme may aim to support. Governance must decide when imbalance is exploitation and when it is solidarity.\n\nF A count of exchanged hours shows activity but not outcome. A weekly visit may reduce isolation even if no concrete task is completed, while a large number of brief transactions may create little lasting connection. Evaluators therefore combine ledger data with interviews, retention and evidence of new relationships. The writer rejects the idea that rising transaction volume alone proves success. Measurement should follow the scheme's stated purpose, whether that is mutual aid, skills access or community participation.\n\nG Time banks often become more stable when hosted by a library, health service or community centre. An anchor can supply rooms, safeguarding procedures and a coordinator. Dependence on one sponsor, however, makes the network vulnerable to a budget cut or a change of priorities. Diverse partnerships and member-led governance spread that risk. Institutional support is most useful when it provides continuity without converting the bank into a programme in which professionals deliver and residents merely receive.\n\nH Time credits can reveal abilities ignored by conventional markets and create low-cost routes into participation. They cannot replace adequate public services or income. Treating volunteer hours as a substitute for trained care may transfer responsibility onto people with the fewest resources. The strongest claim is narrower: within clear limits and with dependable coordination, a time bank can build reciprocal relationships that neither cash purchase nor unstructured volunteering produces in quite the same way.\n\nI Joining procedures shape who participates. Lengthy checks can deter casual offers of help, but no screening may expose vulnerable members to risk. Clear service descriptions, references for sensitive roles and a simple orientation provide a middle route. Schemes also need an exit policy for unused credits. If balances vanish without explanation, members may feel that earlier contributions were devalued; if they remain forever, the ledger becomes crowded with obligations the network cannot fulfil.\n\nJ Unsuccessful exchanges are valuable evidence when coordinators record why they collapsed. The obstacle may be travel distance, unclear expectations or a mismatch between requested and available times. Treating every failed match as personal unreliability hides design problems. Patterns can guide new meeting points, training or narrower promises. In this sense, administration does more than count hours: it translates repeated friction into changes that make future reciprocity more achievable.",
